<h2>Why the affidavit carries so much weight</h2>
<p>Interim maintenance is decided without trial, on affidavits and documents. The court cannot audit a business or trace benami holdings at that stage; it can only compare the sworn disclosure against objective markers — bank statements, income tax returns, rent, school fees, vehicles, travel. The affidavit is therefore not a formality but the evidentiary foundation of the order, and both the trial courts and the High Court of Delhi treat its accuracy as a matter going to the administration of justice, not merely to the parties' private contest.</p>

<h2>The concealment playbook — and the judicial counter-moves</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>The concealment.</strong> Salary routed partly in cash; business income shown at survival level while the family runs cars and private schooling; property held in a parent's or sibling's name; resignation from employment timed to the maintenance petition; the claimant's own earnings or rental income omitted; qualifications suppressed to resist earning-capacity arguments.</div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>The counter-move.</strong> Adverse inference from lifestyle; income attribution at a realistic figure; directions to produce bank statements, ITRs, GST returns and Form 26AS; comparison across the affidavit's own internal entries; costs for amended or corrected affidavits; in stubborn cases, striking of defence and fixation on the other side's material.</div>
</div>

<h2>The escalation ladder</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Adverse inference.</strong> The workhorse response: where disclosure is implausible, the court assesses income notionally — against expenditure, lifestyle and the standard of living the household maintained during cohabitation.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Directions and costs.</strong> Courts direct fresh or supplementary affidavits, production of specific documents, and impose costs for deficient disclosure — small sanctions with large signalling value.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Procedural sanction.</strong> Defiance of repeated directions can cost the defaulter the right to be heard on quantum — the defence is effectively closed and the order goes on the compliant party's material.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Perjury exposure.</strong> A sworn affidavit that is deliberately false is an offence against public justice. Courts can direct proceedings on their own complaint in appropriate cases; the exposure is real even if prosecutions are reserved for egregious cases.</div>
</div>

<h2>What honest litigants should do</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Disclose first, argue second.</strong> A complete affidavit with an argument about why the income cannot sustain the claimed amount fares far better than a thin affidavit — credibility is the currency of interim proceedings.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Attack concealment with documents.</strong> The opposing party's concealment is proved by paper: bank summonses, ITR production directions, employer records, society and school records. A rhetorical accusation of concealment achieves nothing; a contradiction between the affidavit and a bank statement achieves everything.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Update when circumstances change.</strong> Income changes during litigation — new employment, business downturns, retirement. Filing an updated disclosure before the other side discovers the change preserves credibility; being caught preserves nothing.</div>
</div>

<h2>The larger point</h2>
<p>Maintenance jurisprudence has moved decisively toward disclosure-driven adjudication: standardised affidavits, both parties bound, lifestyle benchmarks in place of speculation. In that architecture, the false affidavit is not a tactic but a self-inflicted wound — it converts a quantum dispute the litigant might partly win into a credibility contest the litigant has already lost. The most valuable advice a maintenance litigant receives is often the least welcome: state the finances truthfully, and fight the case on the law and the arithmetic.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>This article describes general practice in maintenance proceedings before Delhi courts. It is informational and not advice on any specific matter; disclosure formats and directions vary with the forum and the stage of the case.</p>
</div>
